  <span class="eyebrow">Industry insight</span>
  <h1>B2B explainer video: making complex products clear</h1>
  <p class="lead">B2B buyers want to understand what they are buying, but complex technology and processes are hard to explain. Explainer videos solve that: they break complicated material into steps people follow, lift conversion and defuse objections before they reach your sales team. Swiss industrial and financial companies — UBS, Siemens, Porsche among them — use video to carry their B2B message clearly. This article shows how to use explainer videos with a plan rather than by reflex.</p>
  <p class="post-meta"><span>Sofia Treviño</span> <span>·</span> <span>August 10, 2026</span></p>
</div></div></section>
<section class="sec" style="padding-top:40px"><div class="wrap"><div class="post-wrap post-body reveal">
<img src="/projects/carvolution-tvc-social-media-campaign/06-sec_EN_B_v20210824_COMPOSED.Still0011.jpeg" alt="B2B explainer video: making complex products clear" loading="eager" style="width:100%;border-radius:16px;margin:8px 0 26px;aspect-ratio:16/9;object-fit:cover" />
<h2>Why explainer videos matter for B2B products</h2><p>In B2B, time is the scarcest thing in the room. Decision-makers have little patience for long documentation. A well-made explainer video does in 90 to 180 seconds what a ten-minute presentation cannot: it shows the core of a solution so the benefit lands immediately.</p><p>Research puts video recall as much as 80 percent above text. For technical or abstract products that means something concrete: prospects understand your solution faster, ask better questions and arrive closer to a decision. It also cuts support load, because the questions everyone asks are already answered.</p><h2>The four kinds of B2B explainer video</h2><ul><li><strong>Product videos:</strong> features and use cases in action. Best for software, machinery or services. <a href={p("services/product-video")}>More about product videos</a>.</li><li><strong>Process and how-to videos:</strong> steps explained in order. Made for onboarding, training and implementation. <a href={p("services/how-to-video")}>How-to videos for your training</a>.</li><li><strong>Concept videos:</strong> abstract ideas made visible through animation and graphics. Particularly effective for complex B2B concepts or business models.</li><li><strong>Customer testimonials:</strong> other companies showing how they use your product. In B2B, credibility is built by other people, not by you.</li></ul><h2>What separates a good one: story, not a feature list</h2><p>The commonest mistake in B2B explainer video is listing features instead of solving a problem. An effective video answers the customer&rsquo;s question — &laquo;why would I need this?&raquo; — not yours: &laquo;what can it do?&raquo;</p><p>The structure that works:</p><ul><li><strong>Hook (0–5 seconds):</strong> a real problem or question that earns the next ten seconds.</li><li><strong>Context (5–30 seconds):</strong> why the problem matters, and who has it.</li><li><strong>Solution (30–120 seconds):</strong> how your product solves it, concretely, with examples or visualisation.</li><li><strong>Call to action (120–180 seconds):</strong> one clear next step — request a demo, book a call, download the paper.</li></ul><p>Swiss companies tend to lean on a particular register: reliability, quality, efficiency. Those values land here in a way that hype does not.</p><h2>Where B2B explainer videos are used</h2><p>An explainer video is not a finished asset — it is one piece of a wider strategy. The usual places:</p><ul><li><strong>Website and landing pages:</strong> video on the home or product page lifts time on page and conversion.</li><li><strong>Sales enablement:</strong> sales teams use video inside calls or by email, and save the explanation each time.</li><li><strong>LinkedIn and social:</strong> <a href={p("services/social-media-video")}>shorter cuts for social media</a> earn reach and engagement.</li><li><strong>Webinars and events:</strong> an explainer at the start removes the basic questions and leaves room for the real discussion.</li><li><strong>Training and onboarding:</strong> after the sale, video gets new users productive sooner.</li></ul><h2>Quality standards for Swiss B2B production</h2><p>Professional video is an investment. In B2B, quality is not negotiable: a quickly-made video with poor audio or dated animation costs you credibility rather than buying it.</p><p>What actually matters:</p><ul><li><strong>Clear language and timing:</strong> no marketing jargon. Short sentences, and pauses that earn their place.</li><li><strong>Professional voiceover:</strong> a native speaker, in the right register — in B2B, serious beats funny.</li><li><strong>Strong graphics and animation:</strong> consistent with your brand. A distinctive visual style is what gets remembered.</li><li><strong>Technical execution:</strong> 1080p or 4K, built for mobile and desktop, properly colour-graded.</li></ul><p><a href={p("services/corporate-video")}>More about how we produce video</a> and how we bring your message to life.</p><h2>Measurement and ROI</h2><p>Video should not be produced on instinct. Measure:</p><ul><li>How long people watch. Drop-off tells you exactly where you lose them.</li><li>Which cut converts better — 90 seconds or 180.</li><li>Which audience engages, from LinkedIn and YouTube analytics.</li><li>Whether support load falls and lead quality rises after the video goes live.</li></ul><p>What we see with Swiss companies is consistent: the time to a decision gets shorter, and satisfaction after the sale goes up.</p><h2>Video is no longer a nice-to-have</h2><p>B2B buyers expect video now. It stopped being a differentiator and became a baseline, much like having a site that works on a phone. The companies that explain complex products clearly and professionally earn trust and close faster.</p><p>Your expertise deserves communication that matches it. If you are ready to tell your product visually, <a href={p("contact")}>talk to our team</a>. We know B2B in the Swiss context — and how to make technology understandable.</p>
